UP Health
A Diagnostic Tool for Universal Profile Security & Configuration
Version: 2.0 (Final Proposal — Post-Research)
Status: Ready for Build Planning
Date: June 28, 2026
Builder: Solo Developer, $0 Budget
1. Executive Summary
UP Health is a read-only diagnostic tool for LUKSO Universal Profiles. A user provides any
Universal Profile address (by pasting it, or by connecting their wallet) and receives a plainlanguage breakdown of who controls that profile, what each controller is actually capable of
doing, and whether anything about the current setup looks risky, stale, or misconfigured.
The tool does not manage, edit, or fix anything. It diagnoses. For remediation, it directs
users to the existing LSP6 permission manager already available in the LUKSO ecosystem
(via lukso.tools).
One-line description: “Paste a Universal Profile address. Find out exactly who can do what
to it, and whether anything looks risky.”
The tool is designed to run in two contexts simultaneously: as a standalone website anyone
can visit directly, and as a Mini-App that can be added to a user’s Grid on Universal
Everything, with wallet connection working naturally in both.
2. Background & Motivation
2.1 The Problem Being Solved
LUKSO’s LSP6 Key Manager standard allows a Universal Profile to have multiple
“controllers” — addresses granted specific permissions to act on the profile’s behalf. These
permissions are stored on-chain as bitfields: technically precise, but effectively unreadable
to anyone who isn’t deeply familiar with the standard.
Most Universal Profile holders have no clear, accessible picture of:

Which addresses currently have any level of access to their profile
What each of those addresses can actually do (transfer funds? change profile data? add
new controllers entirely?)
Whether any of that access is unnecessary, forgotten about, or dangerous in
combination
A permission management tool already exists in the ecosystem, allowing users to grant and
revoke permissions through a UI. However, no tool currently exists that clearly diagnoses
the current state of a profile’s permissions before any changes are made. Most available
tooling assumes the user already knows what’s wrong and simply needs an interface to fix
it. UP Health fills the gap before that: helping someone understand what’s actually going on
with their profile in the first place.
2.2 Why This Project, Positioned This Way
This proposal follows two earlier project directions from the same builder, and is
intentionally positioned differently from both:
Universal Index (an ecosystem-wide project discovery platform) was shelved after
discovering that an existing tool, lukso.tools, had already been adopted by LUKSO as the
official in-wallet app store, making direct competition impractical and unnecessary.
Tessera (a soulbound on-chain reputation protocol involving staked LYX, challenge periods,
and slashing logic) was paused — not abandoned — specifically because launching a
project that handles other people’s real financial stakes, as a first public release with no
prior on-chain track record as a builder, carries meaningful reputational and trust risk.
UP Health is deliberately the opposite of Tessera in risk profile. It involves no funds, no
staking, no smart contract deployment, and no custody of anything belonging to a user. It is
read-only by design. The intent is to ship something genuinely useful and low-risk first,
build a visible, credible track record within the LUKSO community, and only then return to
Tessera’s public launch with more established trust and support behind it.
2.3 Why This Is Likely to Be Well-Received
LUKSO’s developer community is small and tightly connected. A tool that helps people
understand and secure their own profile represents a clear public good with no competing
interest and no risk of stepping on another builder’s territory — a direct contrast to the
situation encountered with Universal Index. Even where feedback is critical, it is expected to
be constructive given the size and culture of the community.

3. Core Design Principles
Three principles shape every decision in this proposal:
1. Diagnose, don’t treat. UP Health identifies and explains. It never modifies a profile’s
permissions directly. All remediation is handled by directing the user to existing tools
built for that purpose.
2. Protect third parties by default. Because on-chain permission data is public, anyone
can technically look up anyone else’s profile. UP Health does not assume that everyone
who looks up an address is safe to show sensitive findings to. Full risk information is
only shown to whoever can cryptographically prove, via wallet connection, that they own
the profile in question.
3. Stay lean. No database, no persistent backend state, no custody, minimal
infrastructure. Every piece of data shown is read live from the blockchain at the moment
of the lookup.
4. How the Tool Works
4.1 Core User Flow
1. A user arrives at UP Health, either as a standalone website or as a Mini-App embedded
in their Grid.
2. They either paste a Universal Profile (or Vault) address manually, or connect their wallet.
3. The tool reads the relevant on-chain data for that address.
4. Results are displayed in three layers:
A one-line, plain-language summary at the very top (e.g., “3 controllers found. 1 flag:
a controller other than your main key can add new controllers.”)
A controller-by-controller breakdown, translating each address’s raw permissions
into plain language
A set of risk flags, each with a clear explanation of why it matters and a concrete
next step
4.2 Identity-Matched Access — Not Simple Wallet-Gating
This is a deliberate and important distinction. The gating logic is not “connect any wallet to
unlock more features.” It is: sensitive findings about a specific profile are only revealed

to whoever can prove ownership of that exact profile.
Available to anyone, without connecting a wallet: basic profile information (name and
avatar, if set), the total number of controllers, a general summary of permission activity,
and whether a Universal Receiver Delegate is configured.
Available only when the connected wallet’s address matches the profile being
viewed: the full set of risk flags, staleness/last-activity details, and actionable next
steps.
If someone connects their wallet but looks up a different profile than their own, they still
only see the basic, non-sensitive tier of information. The gate checks for a match between
the connected identity and the profile being examined — not merely the presence of any
connection.
This design exists specifically so the tool cannot be used as a public vulnerability-disclosure
mechanism against a stranger’s profile. The underlying data is public on-chain regardless,
but UP Health itself will not surface risk framing to anyone other than the profile’s own
owner.
4.3 Supported Address Types
UP Health supports both Universal Profiles and LSP9 Vaults as lookup targets, using the
same underlying permission-reading logic for both. Vaults use the identical ERC725Y data
storage structure and the same LSP6 Key Manager permission model as Universal Profiles,
so no separate engine is required to support them.
Vault support is included quietly, without dedicated interface elements or messaging
drawing attention to it as a distinct feature. In ordinary use, most people are expected to
look up their own Profile rather than a Vault address directly, so this capability exists for
completeness and for the less common cases where it’s useful, rather than being a headline
feature of the tool.
