Network scope: LUKSO Mainnet only for the initial release. Testnet support is not planned
unless a clear need emerges later.
On the block explorer: LUKSO’s official Mainnet explorer runs on Blockscout, an opensource explorer platform with a standardized, well-documented REST API used consistently
across its many deployments on other networks. This API includes address-level transaction
history with timestamps, which is what powers the staleness indicator. Because
Blockscout’s API shape is consistent software rather than a LUKSO-specific custom build,
its general behavior can be relied upon with reasonable confidence, though the exact
endpoint paths should be confirmed against LUKSO’s specific instance at build time.
6. Features (Confirmed for Initial Release)
6.1 Plain-Language Permission Breakdown
Every controller’s raw LSP6 permission bitfield is translated into a straightforward list of
what that controller can and cannot do. For example:
“This controller can: transfer LYX, call external contracts. This controller cannot: change
your profile data, add new controllers.”
This translation is entirely mechanical. LUKSO publishes an exact, unambiguous mapping
between each permission bit and its corresponding capability, so there is no interpretive
judgment involved in this part of the tool — only accurate decoding.
6.2 Risk Flags (Shown Only to the Verified Owner)
Flags are only displayed once the connected wallet is confirmed to match the profile being
examined. The following flag logic reflects both the project’s own research and LUKSO’s
documented design philosophy for permissions (see Section 7 for the full research findings
behind these rules):
A controller holds permission to add new controllers or edit permissions,

combined with broad execution or value-transfer permissions. Holding the ability to
add controllers on its own is not inherently unusual — LUKSO’s own official accountrecovery design uses a dedicated controller for exactly this purpose. What is flagged
specifically is that same governing power being bundled together with the ability to
move funds or execute arbitrary calls, concentrating too much capability in a single
controller.
Only one controller exists on the entire profile, with no separate recovery path.
LUKSO’s own recommended architecture includes a dedicated, narrowly-scoped
recovery controller as a matter of good practice. A profile with a single point of failure
and no such path is flagged as a real gap.
The Universal Receiver Delegate points to a contract that shows no verifiable code,
or that appears unusually new, treated as its own explicit flag rather than folded into
general permission analysis (see 6.3).
Each flag is presented with a plain-language explanation of why it matters — not simply a
warning label — along with a concrete next step (see 6.5).
6.3 Universal Receiver Delegate Check
This is elevated to its own clearly labeled flag category rather than being buried as
background information. A Universal Receiver Delegate is a contract that runs automatically
when the profile receives assets, so an unusual or unverifiable delegate is a meaningful risk
vector in its own right, deserving the same visibility as a permission-based flag.
6.4 Contract-vs-Wallet Detection
For every controller listed, the tool performs a basic, low-cost check: is this address an
ordinary wallet, or is it a smart contract? This fact is shown for all controllers regardless of
connection status, since it carries no sensitive risk implication on its own — it is simply
useful context.
This is explicitly not the same as multisig detection. It only distinguishes “this is a contract”
from “this is a regular wallet.” True recognition of specific multisig patterns is deferred (see
Section 8).
6.5 Last-Activity / Staleness Indicator
For each controller, the tool shows when it was last active on-chain, sourced from the
Blockscout explorer API described in Section 5. A controller holding sweeping permissions
that has seen no activity in a long time reads very differently from one used recently. This
indicator is descriptive context alongside the flags, not a flag or judgment in itself, and —
like the risk flags — is only shown to the verified profile owner.

6.6 One-Line Summary
A single plain-language sentence sits at the top of every result, giving an immediate read on
the profile’s overall state before the user reads anything else, for example:
“3 controllers found. 1 flag: a controller other than your main key can add new
controllers.”
This is a presentation layer built on top of the same underlying data — it requires no
additional data sources beyond what the other features already gather.
6.7 Actionable Next Steps
Every risk flag shown to a verified owner is paired with a concrete suggested action, and
where relevant, a direct link out to the existing LSP6 permission manager already available
in the ecosystem (via lukso.tools) so the user can act on the finding. UP Health is
intentionally positioned as complementary to that existing tool — one handles diagnosis, the
other handles the fix — rather than as a competing or overlapping product.
7. Research Findings Behind the Risk-Flag Design
Before finalizing the exact flag rules in Section 6.2, two open questions were researched
directly rather than assumed. Both are now resolved with sourced answers.
7.1 What does LUKSO actually recommend for controller setup?
There is no single official document prescribing an exact numeric template (such as “every
profile should have exactly this many controllers with these exact permission sets”).
However, LUKSO’s own infrastructure and standard documentation make the underlying
design philosophy clear and directly usable:
LUKSO’s official account-recovery system is built around a dedicated Recovery Universal
Profile that acts as a controller holding only the permission to add a new controller —
nothing else. It cannot transfer funds, sign transactions, or perform any other action. This
Recovery Profile is itself governed by a multi-signature setup requiring multiple approvals,
with signing keys distributed across secure hardware and multiple environments specifically
so that no single party can unilaterally trigger a recovery.
Separately, the LSP6 standard itself is explicitly designed around granular, narrowly-scoped
permissions, including mechanisms to restrict exactly which contracts a controller may call
and which specific pieces of data it may write. The clear intent, by design, is that controllers
should be scoped as narrowly as possible for their intended purpose, rather than granted
broad, general-purpose access.

This directly shaped two of the flag rules in Section 6.2: the flag logic does not treat “a
controller can add other controllers” as risky in isolation — since that mirrors LUKSO’s own
recommended recovery pattern — but does treat that same capability as risky when
bundled with broad execution or fund-transfer permissions. Separately, a complete absence
of any secondary controller (i.e., no recovery path at all) is treated as a legitimate flag, since
LUKSO’s own architecture clearly recommends against exactly that situation.
7.2 Is multisig-as-controller common enough on LUKSO to matter?
Yes, confirmed. Multisig-controlled addresses acting as controllers are not a hypothetical
edge case — they are already built into LUKSO’s own official recovery infrastructure,
described above, which relies on a Safe (formerly Gnosis Safe) multisig contract governing
the Recovery Profile.
This confirms that reliably recognizing multisig controllers (beyond simply detecting “this is
a contract, not a wallet”) is a genuinely valuable capability, not a low-priority nicety.
However, actually implementing reliable detection of specific multisig patterns is a nontrivial
engineering task in its own right — distinct from the research question of whether it matters.
For this reason, basic contract-versus-wallet detection ships in the initial release (Section
6.4), while true multisig-specific recognition is deferred to a later phase once real usage
patterns and priorities are better understood (see Section 8).
7.3 Does LSP9 Vault support belong in the initial release?
Yes. LSP9 Vaults are, structurally, ERC725Y-based contracts using the exact same data
storage schema and the same LSP6 Key Manager permission model as Universal Profiles.
This means the entire permission-reading and decoding engine built for Universal Profiles
works identically for Vaults, with no separate logic required.
Given how little additional work this requires, Vault support is included in the initial release
rather than deferred, but without any dedicated interface or messaging drawing attention to
it — see Section 4.3.
8. Explicitly Out of Scope for the Initial Release
The following were deliberately excluded from the first release to keep scope lean,
shippable, and low-risk. Each is a candidate for a later phase, once the tool has real usage
and community feedback to inform the decision, not because any of them are considered
unimportant:
1. True multisig-pattern detection. Recognizing specific multisig implementations (such
as Safe) as a distinct category from other smart-contract controllers, beyond the basic

contract-versus-wallet fact already included.
2. Ecosystem-wide baseline comparisons. For example, showing a user how their
profile’s setup compares to typical setups across the ecosystem. Building this honestly
requires real usage data to establish any legitimate baseline; inventing one now would
amount to guessing.
3. Historical tracking over time. Showing users how their permissions have changed
since a previous check would require storing snapshots persistently, which reintroduces
a database and ongoing infrastructure — directly contrary to the tool’s lean, stateless
design principle.
9. What This Tool Explicitly Does Not Do
It does not send any transactions or make any writes to the blockchain — it is fully readonly.
It does not require a wallet connection for basic lookups — pasting an address alone is
sufficient for the non-sensitive information tier.
It does not take custody of funds, involve staking, or contain any financial logic of any
kind.
It does not edit or fix permissions directly. It always directs users to the existing,
purpose-built permission manager tool for that.
It does not store looked-up addresses server-side beyond, optionally, basic anonymized
usage analytics.
It does not attempt cross-profile comparison or ecosystem-wide benchmarking in this
release.
10. Technical Approach
This section describes the intended technical approach in plain terms, without prescribing
exact code or implementation details, so that any builder picking this up can make informed
decisions during actual development.
10.1 General Architecture
UP Health is a frontend-only application. There is no database and no persistent backend
service. Every result shown to a user is fetched live from the blockchain (and the block

explorer, for activity history) at the moment of the lookup. This keeps the tool simple to
maintain, cheap to run, and free of any need to keep stored data in sync with on-chain
reality.
10.2 Reading On-Chain Data
Reading a profile’s permission data, metadata, and Universal Receiver Delegate
configuration is done directly against a LUKSO Mainnet RPC endpoint, using the standard
ERC725Y data-key structure that LSP6 and LSP3 define. This is a well-documented,
standard pattern already used throughout the LUKSO ecosystem, and does not require any
custom indexing infrastructure.
10.3 Reading Transaction History for Staleness
Because determining “when was this controller last active” from raw blockchain data alone
can be slow or impractical for addresses with long histories, this feature relies on LUKSO’s
official Mainnet block explorer (a Blockscout-based explorer) and its public API, rather than
scanning blockchain history manually. The exact API endpoint structure should be
confirmed against LUKSO’s specific explorer instance at the time of implementation, though
Blockscout’s API conventions are consistent and well-documented across its many
deployments on other networks, which gives reasonable confidence in this approach ahead
of time.
10.4 Wallet Connection — Two Contexts, One Experience
A specific and important design requirement for this tool is that it must work naturally in two
different contexts: as a Mini-App embedded inside a user’s Grid on Universal Everything,
and as an entirely standalone website that anyone can visit directly by URL. These two
contexts require genuinely different connection handling, not a single mechanism that
transparently covers both, and this distinction should be understood clearly by whoever
builds this:
When embedded in a Grid: LUKSO provides a specific mechanism (the up-provider
package) built exactly for this situation, allowing a Mini-App to detect the profile whose Grid
it’s sitting in, and to let a visitor connect with a single click, brokered by the parent page
itself.
When visited as a standalone website: There is no parent page to broker anything, and no
Grid context exists at all in this scenario. Wallet connection in this case follows the same
general pattern as any standard web3 application connecting to a browser-based wallet
extension — the user’s Universal Profile Browser Extension acts as the connection point,
following the same conventions LUKSO’s own official standalone-app templates use.
The practical implication is that the application needs to determine which of these two

contexts it’s running in, and connect accordingly — but once a connection is established
through either path, the same underlying permission-reading logic and the same results
display applies identically regardless of how the user arrived. From the user’s perspective,
the experience should feel seamless and unified; underneath, two different connection
handshakes are quietly supporting that single experience.
10.5 Hosting and Cost
Consistent with this builder’s established approach across other projects, UP Health is
intended to run entirely on free-tier infrastructure — a standard web hosting platform
capable of serving a modern frontend application, with no paid services required for the
scope described in this proposal.
11. Naming
The tool is named UP Health.
An earlier placeholder name, “UP Doctor,” was considered and deliberately set aside.
“Doctor” implies active treatment, which misrepresents what this tool actually does — it
diagnoses and directs elsewhere for remediation, but never treats directly. “Health,” used as
a noun, more honestly reflects the tool’s actual scope: reporting a state, not acting on it.
12. Success Criteria for the Initial Release
The tool correctly and accurately decodes LSP6 permissions for any valid Universal
Profile or Vault address.
Identity-matched access control works correctly: a verified owner sees full risk flags and
next steps, while anyone else sees only the basic, non-sensitive information tier.
The confirmed risk flags (Section 6.2) are accurate and framed constructively, avoiding
unnecessary alarm.
Actionable next steps correctly link out to the existing, established permission manager
tool in the ecosystem.
The tool functions correctly and identically in both supported contexts: as a standalone
website, and as a Mini-App embedded in a user’s Grid.
The tool is shared publicly within the LUKSO community and gathers real feedback to
inform later phases.

No false-positive flag unfairly alarms a user about the safety of their own profile.
